ot sargable.** A plain index on `users.email` can't be used, so the planner scans the whole `users` table and computes `lower()` on every row. This is the biggest issue.\n   - Fix, option A: create an expression index, `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. The query then matches it as written. This works in Postgres, and in MySQL 8.0.13+ with a functional index.\n   - Fix, option B: store emails normalized to lowercase and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain unique index. In Postgres you can also use the `citext` type. In MySQL, a case-insensitive collation already does this.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` over a join.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide or text columns. It also blocks index-only scans, and it returns duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at`, and so on) that can break clients. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **Probable missing index on `orders.user_id`.** I can't see the schema, so I'm assuming here. If `orders.user_id` has no index, each matching user triggers a scan of `orders`. Postgres doesn't create indexes on foreign keys automatically. Check with `\\d orders`, and add `CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id);` if it's missing.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or pagination.** If a user can have many orders, the result is unbounded. This matters if the query backs an API endpoint.\n\n**Suggested rewrite**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nUse the columns you actually need. This needs the two indexes above, plus `orders (user_id, created_at)` if you keep the `ORDER BY`.\n\nI only had the query text. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on real data to confirm the plan, and send me the schema if you want the index advice checked against it.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}